22:01:58 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: That’s a great question—and you’ve hit on one of the most fascinating chapters of tech history. First, a small correction: not *everyone* thought the Internet was a fad. But in the early to mid-1990s, there was a loud and influential chorus of skeptics—journalists, economists, even tech insiders—who dismissed it as overhyped, impractical, or a bubble bound to burst. Here’s why so many felt that way. ### 1. The Internet was painfully slow and hard to use In the early ’90s, going online meant dial-up modems that screeched and tied up the phone line. Web pages were text-heavy, graphics loaded line-by-line, and a single image could take minutes to appear. For most people, the experience was more frustrating than magical. It felt like a downgrade from real life, not an upgrade. ### 2. It looked like a niche hobby, not a mass‑market tool The Internet’s early adopters were academics, government researchers, and a handful of tech enthusiasts. Email and Usenet were the killer apps, but they seemed irrelevant to everyday people who already had phones, fax machines, and postal mail. As late as 1995, only about 14% of American adults had used the Internet at all. It was easy to dismiss as a sandbox for geeks. ### 3. There was no clear business model Companies scrambled to build “brochureware” websites, but no one had proven you could make real money online. Secure online payments were nascent, and the idea of buying goods without seeing them seemed absurd to most consumers. In 1998, economist Paul Krugman famously wrote, “The growth of the Internet will slow drastically… By 2005 or so, it will become clear that the Internet’s impact on the economy has been no greater than the fax machine’s.” Many business leaders agreed—they saw no path to profitability. ### 4. The dot‑com crash was the “I told you so” moment The late ’90s saw a speculative frenzy: any company with “.com” in its name could raise millions, even if its business plan was vapor. When the bubble burst in 2000, trillions in market value vanished. To skeptics, this wasn’t just a correction—it was proof that the Internet’s promise was empty. After all, if it were truly revolutionary, how could Pets.com fail so spectacularly? ### 5. Media and cultural critics piled on Influential voices like Clifford Stoll, an astronomer and author, wrote a 1995 *Newsweek* piece titled “Why the Web Won’t Be Nirvana.” He argued that online shopping couldn’t replace the tactile experience of a bookstore, that digital news would never be as comfortable as a newspaper, and that virtual communities were poor substitutes for real human contact. It all sounded very level-headed—until it didn’t. ### 6. The Internet was compared to previous overhyped flops The 1990s had seen earlier “revolutions” that fizzled: interactive TV, CD-ROM encyclopedias, virtual reality. For many, the Internet just looked like the next overpromised technology that would soon be forgotten, like the CB radio craze from the ’70s. ### 7. Technical and infrastructure barriers were massive Bandwidth was scarce, and connecting entire nations required enormous investment. Even Bill Gates initially underestimated the Internet’s speed of adoption—his 1995 book *The Road Ahead* barely mentioned the web, focusing instead on proprietary networks like MSN. If the world’s richest tech visionary didn’t fully grasp it, it’s no surprise the average person was skeptical. In hindsight, the skeptics weren’t stupid—they were extrapolating from the present they knew. The Internet of 1995 really *was* a fraction of what it would become. What they missed was the exponential pace of improvement: broadband, Wi-Fi, smartphones, social media, seamless e‑commerce, and platforms that turned the “fad” into the fabric of daily life. The dot‑com crash didn’t kill the Internet; it cleared out the noise and let genuine innovation scale. So while a few saw the potential, the “just a fad” sentiment was a reasonable (if ultimately wrong) judgment based on the technology, business models, and user experience of the time. It’s a powerful reminder that transformative technologies often look like toys at first—until they don’t.
